              {
                "text": "I'm working out the rule by measuring distances from the cross lines at row4, col8, checking how the row and column distances (d_r, d_c) map to output values across the grid.  The pattern seems irregular at first\u2014some combinations give 4, others 0\u2014so I'm tabulating specific (d_r, d_c) pairs like (1,1), (1,2), (2,1) to find what determines when a cell becomes 4 versus 0.\n\nWorking through more rows, the rule clarifies: when d_c exceeds d_r, the cell is 4 if d_c is even; when d_r exceeds d_c, it's 4 if d_r is even. The diagonal case where d_r equals d_c looks trickier since (1,1)=0 but (2,2)=4, so I need to check whether even diagonal values consistently give 4.\n\nTesting this against a 17x17 grid centered at row 3, col 2, I derive that the value equals 4 whenever max(d_r,d_c) is even, otherwise 0, confirming a pattern of concentric squares emanating from the center. I'm now computing the distance values across each column and row to verify this holds throughout the grid.\n\nComputing remaining rows, noticing rows 2 and 4 match, and rows 3, 6 follow the same repeating pattern based on distance from center.\n\nContinuing to compute rows 12-13, then double-checking column 11's distance value against row 13 since it seems miscalculated.\n\nConfirming row11 matches, then working out row12 (d_r=9): zeros for c0-c11, then alternating 4,0,4,0,4 for the remaining columns.\n\nRows 14-16 follow the same pattern: values stay 0 until distance exceeds the row threshold, then alternate 4,0,4 near the diagonal edge.",
